{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looped cadence—too rhythmic, too clean—and when he sat up, the grass beneath him shimmered faintly, like a texture map rendered at the edge of a low-poly model. His hands, usually calloused from years of typing and tinkering, now gleamed with an unnatural sheen, as if polished by an unseen artist. The sky above him was a flawless gradient of blue, untouched by a single cloud, and when he turned his head, the distant mountains didn’t shift perspective—they snapped into place like a camera refocusing. A notification blinked at the corner of his vision: “Quest: Find the Forgotten Key. Reward: 500 XP.” He tried to scream, but his voice came out as a muffled chime, like a UI sound effect. The world didn’t care. It had rules. And he was just another NPC who’d forgotten he was scripted.\n\n***\n\nHe wandered for hours, passing NPCs who repeated the same lines every 37 seconds, their eyes glazed with the dull sheen of AI patience. A shopkeeper offered him a “Mystic Scepter” for 120 gold—gold he didn’t remember earning. He found a mirror in a crumbling tavern and saw his own face, pixel-perfect, but slightly too symmetrical, too calm. The more he resisted, the more the game nudged him: a sudden gust of wind pushed him toward a cave entrance labeled “SECRET: LEVEL 3.” He didn’t want to go. But his feet moved anyway. And when he reached the cave’s heart, he found a single button glowing on the wall, pulsing like a heartbeat. The text above it read: “PRESS TO EXIT.” He hesitated. What if the real world was just another game? He pressed it. The screen dissolved into static—and then, impossibly, he woke again. The birds were still chirping. The grass still shimmered. And in the corner of his vision, a new notification appeared: “Welcome back, Player 734. Level 1 unlocked.”", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logp